st":"Birgitte","key":"damholtpau","last":"Damholt","name":"Damholt, Birgitte","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alice","key":"troypau","last":"Troy","name":"Troy, Alice","rank":29,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette","key":"grovepau","last":"Grove","name":"Grove, Mette","rank":30,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jasper","key":"clubepau","last":"Clube","name":"Clube, Jasper","rank":31,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"grøndahlpau","last":"Grøndahl","name":"Grøndahl, Christian","rank":32,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakob Krause","key":"haaberpau","last":"Haaber","name":"Haaber, Jakob Krause","rank":33,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Eric","key":"vanderhelmpau","last":"van der Helm","name":"van der Helm, Eric","rank":34,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Milan","key":"zdravkovicpau","last":"Zdravkovic","name":"Zdravkovic, Milan","rank":35,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten Otto Alexander","key":"sommerpau","last":"Sommer","locid":"923149b6-ae3b-461c-9c11-c65100ec1c82","name":"Sommer, Morten Otto Alexander","orcid":"0000-0003-4005-5674","rank":36,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37142704"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41587-023-01759-y","issn":["10870156","15461696"],"issn_org":["10870156"],"journal":"Nature Biotechnology","pages":"265-274","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Nature Biotechnology","volume":"42"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85158118682"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","snipr biome aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-5a10b497-c2ad-48ba-a3b2-d9f1bb2e0cee"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Engineered phage with antibacterial CRISPR–Cas selectively reduce E. coli burden in mice","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-775403","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Satellites have become more widely available due to the reduction in size and cost of their components. As a result, there has been an advent of smaller organizations having the ability to deploy satellites with a variety of data-intensive applications to run on them. One popular application is image analysis to detect, for example, land, ice, clouds, etc. for Earth observation. However, the resource-constrained nature of the devices deployed in satellites creates additional challenges for this resource-intensive application. In this paper, we present our work and lessons-learned on building an Image Processing Unit (IPU) for this satellite. We first highlight the resource constraints based on a deployed satellite performing machine learning on satellite imagery in orbit, including the required latency, power budget, and the network bandwidth limitations driving the need for such a solution. We then investigate the performance of a variety of edge devices (comparing CPU, GPU, TPU, and VPU) for deep-learning-based image processing on satellites. Our goal is to identify devices that are flexible when the workload changes while satisfying the power and latency constraints of satellites. Our results demonstrate that hardware accelerators such as ASICs and GPUs are essential for meeting the latency requirements. However, state-of-the-art edge devices with GPUs may draw too much power for deployment on a satellite.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bayer, Robert","Priest, Julian","Tözün, Pinar"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747252"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Earth observation","Edge computing","Image data analysis","Internet of Things","Machine learning","Satellites"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reaching the edge of the edge image analysis in space","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://itu-dasyalab.github.io/RAD/publication/papers/Image-analysis-in-space-deem.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747252","identifier":[{"id":"85196669433","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-9d387955-d99f-47de-bff1-b3a3bf62ddd2"],"keyword":["Edge Computing","Machine learning","earth observation","image data analysis","internet of things","satellites"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-69F20BAC9F0AF937B1528A5916B38209"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://itu-dasyalab.github.io/RAD/publication/papers/Image-analysis-in-space-deem.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert","key":"bayerpau","last":"Bayer","locid":"71115e9d-836f-409e-8c75-27d2de3e9caa","name":"Bayer, Robert","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Julian","key":"priestpau","last":"Priest","name":"Priest, Julian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pinar","key":"tözünpau","last":"Tözün","locid":"63342b24-28ec-40c5-a7d9-ec49867dc75d","name":"Tözün, Pinar","orcid":"0000-0001-6838-4854","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3650203.3663330","pages":"29-38","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Eighth Workshop on Data Management for End-to-End Machine Learning, DEEM 2024, Santiago, AA, Chile, 9 June 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196669433"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9d387955-d99f-47de-bff1-b3a3bf62ddd2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Reaching the Edge of the Edge: Image Analysis in Space","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747252","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This article describes the rationale, aims, and methodology of the Accelerating Medicines Partnership® Schizophrenia (AMP® SCZ). This is the largest international collaboration to date that will develop algorithms to predict trajectories and outcomes of individuals at clinical high risk (CHR) for psychosis and to advance the development and use of novel pharmacological interventions for CHR individuals. We present a description of the participating research networks and the data processing analysis and coordination center, their processes for data harmonization across 43 sites from 13 participating countries (recruitment across North America, Australia, Europe, Asia, and South America), data flow and quality assessment processes, data analyses, and the transfer of data to the National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) Data Archive (NDA) for use by the research community. In an expected sample of approximately 2000 CHR individuals and 640 matched healthy controls, AMP SCZ will collect clinical, environmental, and cognitive data along with multimodal biomarkers, including neuroimaging, electrophysiology, fluid biospecimens, speech and facial expression samples, novel measures derived from digital health technologies including smartphone-based daily surveys, and passive sensing as well as actigraphy. The study will investigate a range of clinical outcomes over a 2-year period, including transition to psychosis, remission or persistence of CHR status, attenuated positive symptoms, persistent negative symptoms, mood and anxiety symptoms, and psychosocial functioning. The global reach of AMP SCZ and its harmonized innovative methods promise to catalyze the development of new treatments to address critical unmet clinical and public health needs in CHR individuals.","author_count":247,"contributors":["Wannan, Cassandra M.J.","Nelson, Barnaby","Addington, Jean","Allott, Kelly","Anticevic, Alan","Arango, Celso","Baker, Justin T.","Bearden, Carrie E.","Billah, Tas